[Music]  welcome to the program alexei navalny  has appeared in court for the first time  since ending a three-week hunger strike  the kremlin critic attended a hearing by  videolink to appeal against his  conviction  in february for defaming a world war ii  veteran  in a separate court case proceedings on  whether to label navalny's opposition  network extremist  have been adjourned it's a move that  could ban the group and lead to  activists facing  long sentences in prison ben and smith  has a very latest for us  from moscow bernard you're outside the  court just tell us what happened there  a short time ago and what's at stake  so prosecutors want to ban navalny's  anti-corruption organization they want  the court to declare it an extremist  organization that  and his regional political groups as  well this would  put navalny's anti-corruption  organization on the same level  as al qaeda meaning people who finance  his group  or supporters of the group could face  very very lengthy jail sentences  and very very heavy prison terms  essentially the court has already issued  an  interim injunction earlier on this week  banning his organization from operating  pending its final  ruling it happened just less than an  hour ago though the court has now  adjourned  the hearing for a few weeks navalny's  lawyers say  they need more time to examine some 14  large  files that the prosecution gave them  only a couple of days ago so the court  has given them  more time to examine those files before  it comes back in session again  but essentially his regional political  groups already closed down  by the prosecutor's office so they  stopped operating  his anti-corruption organization  suspended until the court makes its  final ruling so really  cracking down on all of navalny's  activities  indeed meanwhile the valley did actually  appear  in court in a second case that's also  running what happened there  well this slander case essentially is is  minor  compared to all the other problems that  navalny  faces this was a case this is an appeal  against a conviction for slander and  some  thousand dollar fine when a video was  published  um by uh by the government  showing people voicing their support for  putin's proposed constitutional reforms  navalny called the people taking part in  in that video  traitors and lickspittle and for that  uh he was convicted of defaming a world  war ii  veteran who appeared in that video but  the valni  navalny has been using the opportunity  to address the court  about his conditions about the condition  of russia  the court has not allowed cameras in to  see  that and the judge in fact interrupted  navalny from speaking saying  what he was saying wasn't relevant to  the case  the judge has now gone away to consider  his ruling  in the appeal of that slander case but  all of this  showing how the authorities are chipping  away at navalny  and his organization and when all of  this is concluded  it will just mean that navalny is pretty  much  d very diminished as any sort of serious  political opposition to vladimir putin  and that powerful personality that so  aggravated  the president will be serving a two and  a half year  jail sentence and will be out of sight  bernie smith in moscow thank you  well let's join alex titov now he's a  lecturer in modern european history  specializing in russia at queen's  university uh queensland university in  belfast in northern ireland joins us now  uh from there uh mr titoff good to have  you with us on the program so alexi  uh navalny's supporters preempt the  courts actually by disbanding the  organization  how does this now affect sort of the  legal case brought against him  and the organization considering the  case has just been adjourned  i think that we'll continue with what  they started  and will officially ban it uh so to make  sure that it's not recreated in any  other  shape or form uh there has also been  reports from his  uh supporters from leonid volkov that uh  a new  personal case against navalny and to his  closest associate  volkov and donov being  being made which might see them giving  real sentences just to three of them so  it looks like that there is uh also a  possibility that navalny's  prison sentence will be extended beyond  the two and a half years he's serving  now  what does the move now mean for the  valley in terms of the court cases  specifically  because the court cases don't go away  and as you say you know there's lots of  speculation about those sentences being  increased  well i think that uh i think there's a  very kind of clear consistent  and unediting uh strategy by the kremlin  to a ban to completely eliminate any  navalny presence in regions in russia  put him to jail if necessary and put  give sentences to his closest associates  so they don't even think of returning to  russia  and i think uh so far that it's been uh  working quite well  i have been listening to um lenient  vocals statement of closing down the  original network and uh i still couldn't  see  exactly what they could do uh beyond it  he says there is a group of people who  already  are mature enough to operate on their  own but if  the authorities have are so minded  as to to stop them operating uh even if  they're not formally associated with  navalny  they will so i think they are really in  big trouble uh the  navalny's strategy and i think it's  really again causing the question what  was the  strategy when he came back i think they  did not expect  such a widespread and consistent  crackdown  as they're experiencing now what we have  seen then  certainly in the weeks where navalny has  been incarcerated  is public pressure public moves you  might see the equivalent of a flash mob  uh when when people are called to  protest the public come out  how do you think the russian public are  going to react to this  well i think the protests have been  extremely important to keep  the pressure on authorities and but at  the same time it's also give authorities  extra determination to  uh dismantle the infrastructure for uh  calling those protesting  and if you see they or they've been  changing tactics instead of mass arrests  during the protests  what they've been doing is using all the  face recognition technology  in moscow especially to  identify key people in those protests  out of thousands of people and actually  arrest them after the demonstrations  have been happening  you can see that the pressure pressure  building up on people who  participate in those demonstrations uh  uh  that they will be fired from work  they'll be expelled from universities  and so forth  and i think uh this repression you still  have to remember those  protests although they're large they not  they still tiny  minority of people in action places like  moscow so  uh unless they grow into exponentially  into millions of people on the streets  of moscow alone  uh authorities will be able to handle it  through repression and  intimidation and i think there is no  kind of clear answer what  can be done uh in response to that yet  and you'll have to watch and see alex  titov there from queens university in  belfast  thanks for joining us on al jazeera

